        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people choose a dehumidifier by picking the biggest pint number they can afford. That is the wrong first question. The right order is capacity read correctly, the temperature the machine will live in, and how the water will leave it, and only then efficiency, noise and controls. Get the first three right and almost any reputable unit will do the job; get one of them wrong and no amount of extra capacity will save it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Capacity: Read the Pint Rating the Right Way
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every dehumidifier is sold on a single number: how many pints of water it removes from the air in 24 hours. The trouble is that the number depends entirely on the conditions it was measured in, and in 2019 the Department of Energy changed those conditions. Testing used to happen at 80 degrees Fahrenheit and 60 percent relative humidity. The current test runs at 65 degrees and 60 percent. Cooler air holds less water, so the same machine collects less of it in the new test, and its rating drops accordingly.
          </p>

          <p>
            The size of that drop is roughly a quarter to a third. A unit sold as a 70-pint model in 2018 is, in most cases, the same hardware now sold as a 50-pint. What used to be called a 50-pint unit now carries a rating of around 35. Nothing about the compressor, coil or fan changed; only the label did. This is why a shopper comparing a five-year-old forum recommendation with a current product listing can end up confused, or end up buying a machine far larger than the advice intended.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. If a sizing chart, review or salesperson gives you a pint figure and does not say which rating system it uses, assume it predates 2019 and multiply it by about 0.7 to 0.75 to translate it into current terms. Some manufacturers still print both figures on the box, often with the older and larger number in bigger type, so read the small print. Every capacity figure in this guide is a current, post-2019 DOE rating.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one upside to the change. A basement in summer typically sits somewhere in the low to mid 60s, which means the new 65-degree test is a far better proxy for real basement conditions than the old 80-degree test ever was. A modern pint rating is closer to what a basement unit will actually pull than the old figure, even if in practice a correctly sized machine spends most of its time removing much less than its rating because it is only maintaining a target rather than drying a soaked room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE TRANSLATION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Old 70-pint = new 50-pint. Old 50-pint = new 35-pint.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Same machines, new test conditions. If a recommendation does not say which rating it uses, multiply it by about 0.75 before you shop.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our detailed walkthrough of the numbers, including worked examples for three basement layouts, is in the guide to <a href="/what-size-dehumidifier-for-basement/">what size dehumidifier a basement needs</a>. The short version for everything else is below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="room-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Match Capacity to Room Size and How Damp It Really Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Square footage is only half the sizing answer. The other half is how wet the space is, and most people underestimate it because they judge dampness by what they can see. A room that merely smells musty on humid days needs far less capacity than one with damp patches at the base of the walls, and that in turn needs less than a room where the concrete floor darkens after rain. The same 1,000 square feet can need anything from a 22-pint machine to a 50-pint machine depending on which of those it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the table below as a starting point in current ratings. The classes reflect what the portable market actually sells today: roughly 20 to 22 pints for small units, 30 to 35 pints for the middle class, and 50 pints at the top of the standard portable range. Above that you are into larger commercial-style portables and ducted whole-house units, which is a different purchase with different installation needs.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Space</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Musty only on humid days</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Damp patches or condensation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wet spots or seepage</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedroom or bathroom, under 500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 to 22 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 to 35 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fix the water source first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Room or small basement, 500 to 1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 to 35 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35 to 50 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 pint plus water management</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement, 1,000 to 1,500 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">35 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 pint or whole-house unit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement over 1,500 sq ft or split into rooms</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 pint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Two units or ducted</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Ducted whole-house unit</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            When a room sits between two classes, go up one. A dehumidifier that is slightly too large reaches its target, shuts the compressor off and idles; it costs a little more upfront but no more to run, because it simply runs for fewer hours. One that is too small runs nonstop through July and still loses ground on the worst days. The only real penalty for oversizing is the purchase price and a slightly heavier machine to move.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be honest about the right-hand column. A dehumidifier treats water vapour in the air; it does not stop liquid water coming through a wall or floor. If you have seepage, a crack that weeps after rain, or a floor that is wet rather than just cool, the machine will collect the evaporating water forever and the source will never close. Read <a href="/how-to-fix-a-damp-basement/">how to fix a damp basement</a> before buying, because the grading, gutter or crack repair often costs less than a year of running the unit flat out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Layout matters as much as area. A dehumidifier dries the air it can reach, and air does not move well through doorways and around partition walls. An open-plan basement of 1,400 square feet is one job for one machine. The same area split into a finished room, a utility room and a closed storage room is really three jobs, and a single unit in the finished room will hold that room perfectly while the storage area, usually the dampest part, stays as it was.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperature" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Operating Temperature: Compressor or Desiccant
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every dehumidifier sold for homes is a compressor unit. It works like a small refrigerator turned inside out: a fan pulls air over a cold coil, moisture condenses on the coil and drips into the bucket, and the same air is then warmed over a hot coil before being blown back into the room. It is efficient and cheap to run in warm, damp air, which is exactly what most people are trying to fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Its weakness is cold. As room temperature falls, the coil gets closer to freezing, and once frost forms on it the machine has to stop and defrost before it can collect anything else. Spec sheets for many consumer units list a minimum operating temperature around 41 degrees Fahrenheit, but that is the point at which the machine will still run, not the point at which it still works well. Capacity falls steadily through the 60s and 50s, and in a space that sits in the low 50s a standard compressor unit can spend a large share of its time defrosting.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the space is genuinely cold, you have two better options. The first is a low-temperature compressor unit, the type sold for crawl spaces and unheated basements, which uses a hot-gas or active defrost system so it recovers quickly and keeps working. These cost substantially more and are covered in our <a href="/best-crawl-space-dehumidifier/">crawl space dehumidifier picks</a>. The second is a desiccant dehumidifier, which works on a different principle entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            A desiccant unit passes air over a slowly rotating wheel coated in a moisture-absorbing material and uses a small heater to drive the moisture back out of the wheel into a collection tank. Because it does not rely on a cold coil, it keeps working at temperatures where a compressor unit gives up, and it is lighter and quieter. The trade-offs are real: capacities are smaller, running cost per pint is higher in warm air, and it gives off noticeable heat. That heat is welcome in a cold garage or cellar and a nuisance in a warm bedroom.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Situation</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Why</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basement or room above about 60°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Standard compressor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Most capacity and lowest running cost per pint</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unheated basement or crawl space, 45 to 60°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low-temperature compressor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Active defrost keeps it collecting in cool air</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garage, boat, cabin or cellar near freezing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Desiccant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No coil to frost; adds a little heat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Small warm room where noise matters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Compressor or desiccant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Desiccant is quieter but warms the room</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you are not sure how cold your space runs, put a cheap thermometer down there for a week in spring or autumn before buying. The reading on a cool morning in April tells you more about which machine you need than any sizing chart. Our guide to whether <a href="/do-you-need-a-dehumidifier-in-winter/">you need a dehumidifier in winter</a> covers the cold-season side of this in more detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Drainage: Bucket, Gravity Hose or Built-In Pump
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the spec that most buyers skip and most owners regret. A dehumidifier that has filled its bucket switches itself off, and a switched-off dehumidifier removes nothing. In a damp basement in summer a full-size unit can fill a bucket in well under a day, so whatever capacity you paid for, your real capacity is whatever collects between the times you actually go down and empty it. Decide how the water leaves the machine before you decide which machine to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are three options. Bucket only is fine for a bedroom, a closet or a room that is only mildly humid, and it is fine anywhere you will genuinely empty it daily. Buckets on full-size units commonly hold somewhere around 1.5 to 2 gallons, which is 12 to 16 pints, so a 50-pint unit working hard can need emptying more than once a day. Look for a bucket with a lid and a proper handle; carrying an open tray of water up basement stairs gets old quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gravity drainage is the best option when you can use it. Nearly every unit has a hose connection on the back, usually a standard garden hose thread, and with a hose attached the water runs continuously to a floor drain, sump pit or condensate line. It needs only one thing: the drain must be lower than the outlet, all the way along the hose. Outlets typically sit only a few inches off the floor, so a floor drain or sump pit works and a utility sink does not, unless you raise the unit on a sturdy stand.
          </p>

          <p>
            A built-in pump solves the case where there is no low drain. It pushes the water up and along a thin tube, commonly rated to lift it around 15 feet, so it can reach a utility sink, run up to a window or drain into a laundry standpipe. The cost is a higher price and one more part that can fail, so check that a pump model can also be run in plain gravity mode if the pump ever dies. Our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-with-pump/">best dehumidifiers with a pump</a> compares the models where the pump is worth having.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A full bucket is a zero-pint dehumidifier.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If you cannot drain by gravity and will not empty a bucket every day, buy a pump model. It matters more than the difference between a 35-pint and a 50-pint unit.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you already own a bucket-only unit and have no floor drain, there are workarounds, including a separate condensate pump that sits beside the unit and does the same job as a built-in one. They are covered step by step in <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">how to drain a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a>. A separate pump also has the advantage of being replaceable on its own when it eventually wears out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="efficiency" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Energy Star and What It Actually Costs to Run
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dehumidifiers are not small loads. A full-size portable commonly lists a power draw somewhere in the 500 to 700 watt range on its spec sheet, and in a damp basement in summer it may run many hours a day for months. That makes efficiency worth looking at, and the number that measures it is the Integrated Energy Factor, or IEF, given in liters of water removed per kilowatt-hour of electricity. Higher is better, and because it is measured under the same DOE test as capacity, it is comparable between brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Energy Star certification is a quick filter. Under the current Version 5.0 specification, portable units rated from 25 to 50 pints a day must reach an IEF of at least 1.80 liters per kilowatt-hour to qualify, and units above 50 pints must reach 3.30. Federal minimum standards sit below those thresholds, so a certified unit is meaningfully more efficient than the least efficient units legally on sale. When two machines are otherwise similar, the certified one is the better buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            To estimate running cost, multiply watts by hours run, divide by 1,000 and multiply by your electricity rate. As an illustration, a 550-watt unit running 12 hours a day uses 6.6 kilowatt-hours; at 17 cents per kilowatt-hour that is about $1.12 a day, or about $34 a month. Real hours depend on how damp the space is and what you set the humidistat to, which is why the same machine can cost twice as much to run in one house as in another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two decisions affect the bill more than the badge does. The first is the humidity target: holding 45 percent instead of 50 makes the machine work considerably harder for little real benefit. The second is fixing air leaks and water sources, because every pint that comes in through an open rim joist or a damp wall is a pint you pay to remove. Our breakdown of <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">how much electricity a dehumidifier uses</a> runs the numbers for several common setups.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The Numbers to Look For</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">65°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Current DOE test temperature</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1.80</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Energy Star IEF, 25-50 pint (L/kWh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3.30</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Energy Star IEF, over 50 pint (L/kWh)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~15 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical built-in pump lift</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Noise, and Where the Machine Will Live
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier makes two sounds: the steady rush of the fan and the lower hum of the compressor, which starts and stops as the machine cycles. Manufacturers that publish a noise figure usually give it for the highest fan speed, and full-size compressor units commonly land somewhere in the high 40s to mid 50s in decibels. That is roughly the level of a quiet conversation or a busy office, which is unnoticeable in a basement and very noticeable at night in a bedroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the location should drive how much you care. In an unfinished basement or a utility room, ignore noise entirely and buy on capacity, drainage and efficiency. In a finished basement used as a family room, look for a low fan setting and a unit you can place away from the seating area. In a bedroom, noise becomes one of the top two specs, and a smaller unit on low or a desiccant model is often the better choice; our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">best dehumidifiers for a bedroom</a> covers the quieter end of the market.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compressor cycling is the part people notice most, more than steady fan noise. The click and hum of the compressor restarting every so often is what wakes light sleepers. Some units let you keep the fan running continuously so the change in sound is smaller, while others shut the fan off between cycles. There is no universal answer here, so if a quiet room matters, read owner reviews specifically for comments about the compressor starting and stopping rather than the headline decibel number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement also changes how loud a unit sounds and how well it works. A machine on a bare concrete floor or a hollow wooden platform can transmit vibration, which a simple rubber mat reduces. Leave the clearance the manual asks for, usually several inches to a foot on the intake and exhaust sides, so the fan is not straining against a wall. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-dehumidifier-in-a-basement/">where to place a dehumidifier in a basement</a> goes through the best spots room by room.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bathrooms are a special case. Most portable dehumidifiers are not meant to sit next to a shower, and the real fix for bathroom humidity is usually a correctly sized exhaust fan that is actually run for 20 minutes after a shower. Where a fan is not possible, a small unit can help, and our <a href="/best-bathroom-dehumidifier/">best bathroom dehumidifier</a> guide covers the compact options and the electrical safety points that come with putting one in a wet room.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="controls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Humidistat Accuracy and the Controls Worth Paying For
          </h2>

          <p>
            The humidistat is the sensor that decides when the dehumidifier runs, and it is where cheap and expensive units differ most quietly. You set a target, say 50 percent, and the machine runs until its sensor reads that level. The sensor sits inside the cabinet, often close to the intake, and owners commonly report built-in readings that differ from a separate hygrometer by five points or more. A unit that thinks the room is at 50 percent when it is really at 58 is not doing the job you bought it for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The fix costs very little. Buy a separate digital hygrometer and put it in the middle of the room, away from the dehumidifier, and use its reading rather than the machine display to judge whether the space is dry. If the two disagree consistently, adjust the target on the machine to compensate. This one habit turns any unit into a reasonably accurate one, and it also tells you when something has changed, such as a new leak or a drain that has stopped draining.
          </p>

          <p>
            As for what target to set, most guidance lands between 30 and 50 percent for living spaces, and for basements a setting around 45 to 50 percent keeps mould and dust mites in check without running the machine into the ground. Dry air also makes a basement hostile to moisture-loving pests; we cover how far that goes in <a href="/does-a-dehumidifier-get-rid-of-silverfish/">whether a dehumidifier gets rid of silverfish</a>. The full reasoning on settings is in <a href="/what-should-a-basement-dehumidifier-be-set-at/">what a basement dehumidifier should be set at</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond the humidistat, a handful of features earn their place. Automatic restart after a power cut matters in any space you do not visit daily, because without it a brief outage leaves the machine off until you notice. A washable filter with a reminder light keeps airflow and efficiency up. Continuous mode is useful when draining by hose and you want maximum drying regardless of the reading. Casters and a proper handle matter more than they sound once a 40-pound machine has to be moved.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wi-Fi controls and app scheduling are pleasant rather than essential. The most useful thing an app does is send an alert when the bucket is full or humidity climbs past a threshold, which is genuinely valuable in a basement you rarely visit. Weigh that against the extra cost. Finally, check the warranty: many brands cover the whole unit for a year and the sealed refrigeration system for longer, and a longer sealed-system warranty is a reasonable signal of a maker that expects its compressor to last.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping From a Pre-2019 Sizing Chart</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Old charts quote pint figures from the 80-degree test, which run roughly a third higher than current ratings for the same machine. Following one means buying more capacity than the chart intended. If a source does not say which rating it uses, multiply its figure by about 0.75 before you compare it with anything on sale today.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Bucket-Only for a Damp Basement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A full-size unit in a damp basement can fill its bucket in under a day and then switch off until someone empties it. Unless you can drain by gravity or will genuinely make the trip daily, a pump model or a separate condensate pump is worth more than any extra capacity you could buy instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Standard Unit in a Cold Space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A standard compressor unit in a basement or garage sitting in the low 50s spends much of its time defrosting rather than collecting water. Check the temperature first. Below about 60 degrees, look at low-temperature compressor models, and near freezing, a desiccant unit is the design that actually keeps working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the Built-In Humidity Display</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Built-in sensors sit inside the cabinet and are often several points off. A machine that reads 50 percent while the room is closer to 60 is leaving the space damp. A separate hygrometer in the middle of the room costs very little and tells you what the air is actually doing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Dehumidifier to Cover a Leak</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier removes water vapour; it cannot stop liquid water coming through a crack, a wall or a poorly graded foundation. If floors or walls are visibly wet, the machine will run forever against a source that never closes. Fix gutters, grading and cracks first, then size the unit for what remains.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In current ratings, a mildly humid room under 500 square feet needs about 20 to 22 pints, a damp space of 500 to 1,000 square feet about 35, and a damp basement of 1,000 to 1,500 square feet about 50. Go up a class if you are between two sizes. Oversizing costs a little more upfront but not more to run.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 50-pint dehumidifier really removing 50 pints a day?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only under the DOE test conditions of 65 degrees and 60 percent humidity, running flat out. In a correctly sized installation it removes much less on most days because it is maintaining a target rather than drying a soaked room. If yours is removing close to its rating every day all summer, look for a water intrusion or air leak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a compressor or desiccant dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Compressor for any space that stays above about 60 degrees, because it removes more water per kilowatt-hour. Desiccant for garages, cellars, boats and other spaces near freezing, where a compressor coil frosts up. In between, a low-temperature compressor unit with active defrost is usually the better choice for an unheated basement or crawl space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a built-in pump worth the extra cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you have no floor drain or sump pit below the unit and will not empty a bucket every day. The pump lifts water to a sink, standpipe or window so the machine never stops on a full bucket. If you have a low floor drain, a gravity hose does the same job for free and has nothing to break.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Energy Star matter for a dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is a useful filter. Certified portable units must beat federal minimum efficiency by a clear margin, and because a basement unit can run for hundreds of hours a season, that difference adds up. It matters less than humidity setting and fixing water sources, which change how long the machine runs in the first place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should I set my dehumidifier to?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a basement, 45 to 50 percent is the practical target. It keeps mould, dust mites and moisture-loving insects in check without making the machine run constantly. Going lower costs noticeably more in electricity for little benefit. Check the result with a separate hygrometer, because built-in displays are often several points off.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a whole-house or a portable dehumidifier?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A portable unit is the right answer for one open space up to about 1,500 square feet. A ducted whole-house unit makes sense for large, partitioned or very damp basements, or when you want to dry several floors at once. It costs far more and usually needs professional installation. Our whole-house versus portable guide covers the break-even point.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

