        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Choose in order: capacity read in current ratings and sized for how damp the space really is, the right design for the temperature it will live in, and a drainage plan that means it never stops on a full bucket. Those three decisions account for nearly every dehumidifier that disappoints its owner.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            After that, Energy Star, noise and controls separate a good machine from a merely adequate one. Add a separate hygrometer, set the target to 45 to 50 percent, and fix any liquid water coming in before you ask a dehumidifier to deal with it. Our basement picks are built around exactly those priorities.
          </p>
        </div>

